---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../../components/work/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import CaseColophon from '../../../components/work/CaseColophon.astro';
import ProjectMap from '../../../components/work/diagrams/ProjectMap.astro';
import dashboard from '../../../assets/noll-dashboard.png';
import { ORGS } from '../../../data/site';

// The machine-readable restatement of this page. Every claim below is made in
// plain language somewhere in the body copy.
const work = {
  name: 'Business Intelligence and Analytics, The Noll Team',
  description:
    'Eight months connecting a real-estate brokerage\'s sales, CRM, performance and client data into reporting, analysis and decision support. Michael Doerffler standardized records across nine agents, automated the recurring tracking, and built a Power BI system on a Power Query star schema with DAX measures covering KPIs, monthly trend, and per-agent revenue per connection. The reporting environment covered tens of thousands of client connections a year, more than two hundred home sales as a team, and tens of millions in volume. Segmenting twenty years of transactions found luxury at 11% of sales and 31% of revenue; a staging comparison found staged listings going pending in 9 days against 38. An LLM workflow read four hundred client surveys into themes by agent, and the systems were handed over with SOPs and recorded training.',
  about: [ORGS.noll],
  temporalCoverage: '2025-05/2025-12',
  keywords: [
    'Business intelligence',
    'Data analytics',
    'Power BI',
    'Power Query',
    'DAX',
    'Star schema',
    'Python',
    'Large language model workflow',
    'Sales analytics',
    'Performance reporting',
    'Market segmentation',
    'Real-estate analytics',
  ],
};

const colophon = [
  { label: 'CLIENT', value: 'The Noll Team · Fort Wayne, IN' },
  { label: 'ENGAGEMENT', value: 'First collegiate intern · hired through direct conversation with the CEO' },
  { label: 'ROLE', value: 'AI and Business Strategy Intern' },
  { label: 'TIMELINE', value: 'May to December 2025' },
];

// Three layers, deepening: get the data into a shape worth trusting, make
// performance visible on top of it, then get answers out of it. Deliberately
// not the six business areas again. The map carries the spread, these carry
// what was actually happening underneath it.
const themes = [
  {
    rail: 'Google Sheets · Excel',
    name: 'The data underneath',
    body:
      'The weekly tracking came first. I linked the tabs, wrote the calculation logic, and pointed the whole thing at the Buffini and MLS feeds, then checked every output against numbers the team already trusted. The same job sat under everything else. Nine people kept their records their own way, so I standardized them before I could compare anything.',
  },
  {
    rail: 'Excel · Python · Power BI',
    name: 'Making performance visible',
    body:
      'I built the review structure once, covering KPIs, connections, GCI, and pipeline, then ran it across all nine agents. The Power BI system came later, and nobody asked for it. Power Query feeds a star-schema model, DAX handles the measures, and the report holds a KPI header, a monthly trend, and a per-agent bubble plot behind one filter. The goal and pipeline measures show up exactly as the team already kept them. I surfaced those, I did not redesign them.',
  },
  {
    rail: 'Python · LLM workflow',
    name: 'Turning it into answers',
    body:
      'I standardized connection and revenue records by agent and period, pulled both to the same grain, and worked out what that activity produced in income, for each agent and for the team. The reporting environment covered tens of thousands of client connections a year, more than two hundred home sales as a team, and tens of millions in volume, which is the scale the team was already operating at and had no connected way to read. The surveys were a separate build. An LLM workflow read four hundred PDFs, pulled out recurring themes and sentiment, and sorted the findings by agent, and I wrote the process down so someone else can run it.',
  },
];

const analyses = [
  {
    fig: '31%',
    label: 'OF REVENUE, FROM 11% OF SALES',
    name: 'Should luxury be its own business?',
    body:
      "Brad asked. I segmented twenty years of transaction history against the $500K Fort Wayne benchmark and worked out what share of sales and revenue sat above it. Noll Reserve launched not long after, and the new brand took one of Fort Wayne's highest-priced estates to market and closed it.",
  },
  {
    fig: '9 days',
    label: 'TO SALE, AGAINST 38',
    name: 'Does staging change a sale?',
    body:
      'Noll Team Interiors asked. I compared twelve closings across three zip codes, staged against non-staged inside the same areas so location held roughly constant. The staged homes also closed at 100.8% of list where the others came in at 97.9%.',
  },
];

// The handover, on the three tiers durability actually has: the machine, the
// document, the people. The map above already carries what changed, so nothing
// here restates an outcome.
const outlasted = [
  {
    label: 'STILL RUNNING',
    body: 'The tracking computes itself every week with nobody touching it. The Power BI report refreshes on its own, and the review structure takes any agent who joins.',
  },
  {
    label: 'WRITTEN DOWN AND RECORDED',
    body: 'I wrote SOPs for the workflows, documented the processes, and recorded training videos for the automations and the Power BI system, enough for the next intern to start from, a manager to look back on, or a VA to work from.',
  },
  {
    label: 'WHAT THE TEAM KEPT',
    body: 'Analytics and AI moved past my own desk. I presented findings to the full group, showed people where AI helped and where it did not, and worked through it with agents and virtual assistants.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Data & Analytics in Real Estate · Michael Doerffler"
  description="How I connected sales, CRM, performance and client data into business intelligence, analysis and decision support for a real-estate brokerage over eight months."
  ogImage="/og/noll-team.png"
  breadcrumb="Data & Analytics in Real Estate"
  ogType="article"
  work={work}
>
  <CaseStudyLayout
    caseLabel="THE NOLL TEAM · ANALYTICS & INFRASTRUCTURE"
    eyebrow="REAL ESTATE · DATA & ANALYTICS · AI"
    titleLine1="From data-rich"
    titleLine2="to data-driven."
    standfirst="The Noll Team had built twenty years of records, a CRM full of relationships, and tracking kept faithfully every week. Nobody's job was to turn any of it into a system. I spent eight months building one, a piece at a time."
    nextLabel="FLORIDA DOH"
    nextHref="/work/permit-modernization/"
  >
    <CaseColophon items={colophon} />

    <section class="case-section">
      <p class="situation-stmt" data-reveal>
        A data-rich business with <span class="situation-key">no defined way</span> to use what it
        had.
      </p>
      <p class="situation-sub" data-reveal>
        The role had no template, so I found the work in a loop. Look for what was breaking or
        what leadership wished they could do and couldn't, build it with whoever owned that part
        of the business, then bring back what I found, which almost always turned up the next
        thing worth doing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Breadth. This section shows how far across the business the role got,
         and nothing else: no tools, no build detail, no project names. -->
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2 class="nar-head" data-reveal>Where the work reached</h2>
      <p class="argument" data-reveal>
        The role started in the weekly reporting.
        <span class="argument-key">It did not stay there.</span>
      </p>
      <ProjectMap />
    </section>

    <!-- Depth. Same work, read downward instead of outward: get the data into
         a shape worth trusting, make it visible, then get answers out of it. -->
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2 class="nar-head" data-reveal>What sat underneath</h2>
      <p class="segue segue-lead" data-reveal>
        Six places on the map, but not six separate builds. Each one was built on the last: the
        tracking sheet fed the reporting, the reporting became the dashboard's per-agent view, and
        the sharper questions came out of all of it.
      </p>

      <div class="walk">
        {themes.map((t) => (
          <div class="row" data-reveal>
            <div class="row-rail">
              <p class="rail-tool">{t.rail}</p>
            </div>
            <div class="row-main">
              <h3 class="row-name">{t.name}</h3>
              <p class="row-body">{t.body}</p>
              {t.name === 'Making performance visible' && (
                <figure class="dash">
                  <div class="dash-frame">
                    <Image
                      src={dashboard}
                      alt="The live Power BI dashboard: KPI tiles for total volume, net revenue, sales, connections, and pipeline score; a key-metrics-by-month trend; a monthly revenue-per-connection view by agent; and progress bars toward annual goals. Dollar figures and agent names are redacted."
                    />
                  </div>
                  <figcaption class="dash-cap">
                    THE LIVE POWER BI REPORT · DOLLAR FIGURES AND AGENT NAMES REDACTED
                  </figcaption>
                </figure>
              )}
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <p class="segue" data-reveal>
        The more I showed people what their own numbers could do, the more questions came back the
        other way. Two of them became projects.
      </p>

      <div class="walk">
        {analyses.map((a) => (
          <div class="row" data-reveal>
            <div class="row-rail">
              <p class="rail-fig">{a.fig}</p>
              <p class="rail-figlabel">{a.label}</p>
            </div>
            <div class="row-main">
              <h3 class="row-name">{a.name}</h3>
              <p class="row-body">{a.body}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <h2 class="nar-head" data-reveal>What outlasted it</h2>
      <p class="handoff" data-reveal>
        I built the handoff in from the start, <span class="handoff-key">not onto the end.</span>
      </p>
      <div class="band" data-reveal>
        {outlasted.map((col) => (
          <div class="band-col">
            <h3 class="band-label">{col.label}</h3>
            <p class="band-body">{col.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <h2 class="nar-head" data-reveal>If I started it today</h2>
      <p class="retro" data-reveal>
        I would build one governed source first and let every downstream analysis consume it. Each
        project began by finding, cleaning, and merging much of the same data again; that
        repetition was the cost of building the analytical layer before the foundation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <h2 class="nar-head" data-reveal>The pattern</h2>
      <p class="close-body" data-reveal>
        The tools changed from project to project. The pattern did not: find where information sat
        trapped, make it usable, and carry the answer far enough that someone could act on it.
      </p>
      <p class="close-line" data-reveal>
        The work started with spreadsheets.
        <span class="close-key">The real job was turning information into decisions.</span>
      </p>
    </section>
  </CaseStudyLayout>
</Base>

<style>
  .case-section { margin-top: 9vh; }

  /* The situation: one statement, then one quiet paragraph. No section label. */
  .situation-stmt {
    margin: 2vh 0 0;
    max-width: 900px;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(22px, 2.4vw, 34px);
    line-height: 1.32;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .situation-key { color: var(--accent); border-bottom: 2px solid var(--accent); }
  .situation-sub {
    margin: 3.5vh 0 0;
    max-width: 780px;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1.2vw, 17px);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--ink-muted-2);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* The claim the map and the walk are evidence for */
  .argument {
    margin: 3.5vh 0 0;
    max-width: 900px;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(20px, 2.1vw, 30px);
    line-height: 1.36;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .argument-key { color: var(--accent); }

  /* One rail runs the whole walk. Systems carry a tool label, the two
     analyses carry a figure, and the geometry never changes underneath. */
  .walk { margin-top: 4vh; }
  .row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 168px 1fr;
    gap: 0 3vw;
    padding: 4.4vh 0;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(41, 33, 23, 0.14);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .row:last-child { border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(41, 33, 23, 0.14); }

  .rail-tool {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 10.5px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .rail-fig {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(30px, 3vw, 40px);
    line-height: 1.02;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .rail-figlabel {
    margin: 9px 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 10px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--grey-label);
  }

  .row-name {
    margin: 0 0 10px;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(18px, 1.6vw, 23px);
    line-height: 1.26;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .row-body {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: clamp(14.5px, 1.15vw, 16px);
    line-height: 1.68;
    color: var(--ink-muted-2);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .dash { margin: 3.2vh 0 0; max-width: 720px; }
  .dash-frame { border: 1px solid rgba(41, 33, 23, 0.24); background: #fff; }
  .dash-frame img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; }
  .dash-cap {
    margin-top: 14px;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 10.5px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--grey-label);
  }

  /* Two jobs, one treatment: the handoff from the breadth map into the depth
     below it, and later the reason the last two projects arrived. Same voice,
     so only the runway above them differs. */
  .segue {
    margin: 8vh 0 0;
    max-width: 800px;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(17px, 1.55vw, 22px);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  /* After .segue, whose shorthand margin would otherwise win: this one sits
     directly under a section heading, not between two blocks of rows. */
  .segue-lead { margin-top: 3vh; }

  .handoff {
    margin: 3vh 0 0;
    max-width: 820px;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(19px, 1.9vw, 27px);
    line-height: 1.35;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .handoff-key { color: var(--accent); }

  .band {
    margin: 4vh 0 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 0 3.5vw;
    padding-top: 4vh;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(41, 33, 23, 0.2);
  }
  .band-label {
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 10.5px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.15em;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .band-body {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: clamp(13.5px, 1.1vw, 15px);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink-muted-2);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* Demoted on purpose: real judgment, but not the note to end on */
  .retro {
    margin: 3vh 0 0;
    max-width: 660px;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: clamp(14.5px, 1.15vw, 16px);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--grey-label);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* The crescendo: the principle, then the line the page ends on */
  .close-body {
    margin: 3vh 0 0;
    max-width: 780px;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1.2vw, 17px);
    line-height: 1.68;
    color: var(--ink-muted-2);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .close-line {
    margin: 5vh 0 0;
    max-width: 900px;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(23px, 2.6vw, 38px);
    line-height: 1.28;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .close-key { color: var(--accent); }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .band { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 3.5vh 0; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .case-section { margin-top: 7vh; }
    .row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 1.6vh 0; padding: 3.6vh 0; }
    .band { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 3.5vh 0; }
  }
</style>
